# See https://help.github.com/articles/ignoring-files/ for more about ignoring files.

# dependencies
/node_modules
# The leading slash above anchors to the repo root, so it does NOT cover
# vscode-extension/node_modules — 20 MB of vendored TypeScript that a
# `git add -A` would otherwise commit. .dockerignore already says
# `**/node_modules`; this makes git agree.
**/node_modules/
/.pnp
.pnp.*
.yarn/*
!.yarn/patches
!.yarn/plugins
!.yarn/releases
!.yarn/versions

# testing
/coverage

# next.js
/.next/
/out/

# production
/build

# misc
.DS_Store
*.pem

# debug
npm-debug.log*
yarn-debug.log*
yarn-error.log*
.pnpm-debug.log*

# env files (can opt-in for committing if needed)
.env*
# ...except the template, which contains no values and documents the setup.
!.env.production.example

# Legacy provider store.
#
# providers.json holds API keys in PLAINTEXT. It predates the encrypted
# per-user credential store and is only read once, by importLegacyProvidersFor,
# to migrate old installs. It was untracked but not ignored, so a single
# `git add .` would have published live keys. Never commit it.
providers.json

# The same mistake wearing a different name.
#
# user_local.env is written by the settings panel and holds a live
# OMNIROUTE_API_KEY. The `.env*` rule above does NOT match it: that pattern
# anchors on a name that STARTS with `.env`, and this one starts with `user`.
# So the file sat here tracked-by-nothing but ignored-by-nothing, and the first
# `git add -A` before pushing to a deploy remote would have published the key.
# (.dockerignore already excluded it, which is why it never reached an image.)
user_local.env
*.env
!.env.production.example

# Local database and its WAL sidecars. Contains user accounts, sessions,
# encrypted credentials and chat history.
chat.db
chat.db-shm
chat.db-wal
chat.db.backup-*
*.sqlite
*.sqlite3

# Local agent state and the app's own rolling backups.
/.agent/
/.omniroute-backups/

# Rendered PDFs and Word documents, written at runtime by
# src/app/api/chat/generate-document/route.ts. These are other people's
# documents: the directory is shared by every account and the filenames are the
# document titles. `.dockerignore` now excludes it too.
/generated-documents/
/public/generated-pdfs/

# Deep Cowork's context layer: 121 files of task notes, journals and a
# workspace.json recording an absolute path on this machine. `.dockerignore`
# already excluded it; this makes git agree. It is regenerated on demand, it is
# meaningless on another machine, and the task filenames alone are a transcript
# of what was being debugged.
/.omniroute/

# SSH keys for the deployment box.
*.pem
*.ppk

# Manual backup copies kept beside the source files.
#
# There are 19 of these, and eight sit INSIDE src/app/api — pre-hardening
# copies of the chat, models, chats, pdf and omniroute/debug handlers. None of
# them contains a key (checked), and none of them can serve traffic: Next.js
# only routes a file named exactly `route.ts`, and tsconfig.json already
# excludes every one of these names from type-checking, so the build does not
# see them either. What they are is a published map of the old handlers, back
# when several of them had no guard at all — the thing an attacker reads first.
# They were tracked by nothing and ignored by nothing, so the first `git add -A`
# would have committed all 19.
#
# The patterns are written narrowly on purpose. A tempting `*backup*` would also
# match `src/components/BackupsPanel.tsx`, which is LIVE code for the in-app
# backup UI, and silently drop it from the next commit. Every pattern below
# therefore requires the underscore or hyphen that only the copies have.
*_backup.*
*backup_*.*
backup_*.*
smart-backup*
# One file is misspelled with a bracket instead of an `e`, so no pattern above
# reaches it. tsconfig.json excludes it by this same literal path.
src/app/api/omniroute/debug/reout)backup.ts

# vercel
.vercel

# typescript
*.tsbuildinfo
next-env.d.ts
